      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we need to analyze the outcomes for each die and then combine them.\n\nFirst, consider the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die. A six-sided die has the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. The numbers less than three are 1 and 2. Therefore, there are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes. The probability \\( P_1 \\) is given by:\n\\[\nP_1 = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die. The numbers greater than three are 4, 5, and 6. Therefore, there are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes. The probability \\( P_2 \\) is given by:\n\\[\nP_2 = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the rolls of the two dice are independent events, the combined probability of both events happening (rolling a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die) is the product of the individual probabilities:\n\\[\nP(\\text{combined}) = P_1 \\times P_2 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]",
